---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevEncodeHash from '@/components/tools/security/DevEncodeHash.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="hmac-generator">
  <DevEncodeHash client:load algorithm="HMAC" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>What HMAC adds to a hash</h2>
    <p>
      A plain hash does not prove who made it, because anyone can compute one. An HMAC generator mixes a
      shared secret into the hash in a specific nested construction:
    </p>
    <pre><code>HMAC(K, m) = H((K ⊕ opad) ‖ H((K ⊕ ipad) ‖ m))</code></pre>
    <p>
      The two padded copies of the key make it secure: the construction is provably resistant to
      length-extension attacks. So use HMAC, and do not hash a secret and a message together.
    </p>

    <h2>Debugging a webhook signature</h2>
    <p>
      When a provider says your signature does not match, the cause is almost always one of these, most
      likely first:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        The body is not the raw body. Your framework parsed the JSON and serialised it again, with a
        different key order or whitespace. Capture the bytes before any parsing.
      </li>
      <li>
        A prefix is missing. Stripe signs <code>timestamp + "." + payload</code>. GitHub signs the body
        alone but puts <code>sha256=</code> before the header value. Read the exact construction in the
        provider's docs.
      </li>
      <li>
        The key format is wrong. A secret that looks like hex can be meant as UTF-8 text, or the
        reverse. Try all three formats in the selector above and see which gives the expected signature.
      </li>
      <li>
        The output encoding is different. Some providers send hex, others Base64. The values look
        different but can be the same bytes.
      </li>
      <li>
        A trailing newline. One extra byte changes the whole code.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Common provider constructions</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Provider</th><th>Algorithm</th><th>Signed value</th><th>Output</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Stripe</td><td>HMAC-SHA256</td><td><code>t.payload</code></td><td>Hex</td></tr>
          <tr><td>GitHub</td><td>HMAC-SHA256</td><td>Raw body</td><td>Hex, prefixed <code>sha256=</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Shopify</td><td>HMAC-SHA256</td><td>Raw body</td><td>Base64</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Slack</td><td>HMAC-SHA256</td><td><code>v0:timestamp:body</code></td><td>Hex, prefixed <code>v0=</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>AWS SigV4</td><td>HMAC-SHA256, chained</td><td>A derived key over four stages</td><td>Hex</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Always check the provider's current documentation. These constructions change, and the bugs are
      in the details.
    </p>

    <h2>Comparing signatures safely</h2>
    <p>
      In production code, never compare with <code>==</code>. A byte-by-byte comparison returns early
      on the first mismatch. Over enough requests the timing difference is measurable, and an attacker
      can recover a valid signature one byte at a time. Use your platform's constant-time
      comparison: <code>hmac.compare_digest</code> in Python,
      <code>crypto.timingSafeEqual</code> in Node, <code>hash_equals</code> in PHP.
    </p>
    <p>
      The comparison on this page is a debugging aid, so a plain equality check is fine here.
    </p>

    <h2>Also verify the timestamp</h2>
    <p>
      A valid signature does not make a request fresh. Without a timestamp check, an attacker who
      captured one webhook can replay it forever. Reject requests older than a few minutes, and put the
      timestamp in the signed value so nobody can change it. That is why Stripe and Slack sign it with
      the body.
    </p>

    <h2>Pasting a production secret</h2>
    <p>
      The secret stays in this tab: WebCrypto runs locally, and the page cannot make a network request.
      Use a test key if you have one, because a screenshot tool or a person behind you can see the
      screen.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
